  "unit": "u07-directions",
  "title": "Left, right, straight",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Directions use the polite command **যান** (*jan*) 'go': **ডানে যান** 'go right', **বামে যান** 'go left', **সোজা যান** 'go straight'. The same words also say where something is: **হোটেল বামে** 'the hotel is on the left'.\n\n**মোড়** (*mor*) — intersection, corner — is the key landmark word in Bangladeshi cities. With the locative it becomes **মোড়ে** 'at the intersection': **মোড়ে বামে যান** 'turn left at the intersection'.\n\nTwo little words after a noun:\n\n- **দিয়ে** 'along, through': **এই রাস্তা দিয়ে সোজা যান** 'go straight along this road'\n- **থেকে** 'from': **এই মোড় থেকে ডানে যান** 'turn right from this intersection'\n\n**Culture:** street names and house numbers won't get you far in Dhaka. People find their way by landmarks — a big intersection (whole neighbourhoods are known by their মোড়, like Shahbag or Farmgate), a mosque, a well-known shop. Expect directions in short steps — 'go straight to the intersection, then ask again' — and don't hesitate to ask a shopkeeper or tea-stall owner.\n\n**Kolkata:** people more often say **ডানদিকে** / **বাঁদিকে** ('to the right side / left side').",
